1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,795
Imagine waking up tomorrow to the news that Yellowstone,

2
00:00:03,795 --> 00:00:07,183
the famous national park in the United States, has

3
00:00:07,183 --> 00:00:11,046
erupted. Not a small eruption. A super-eruption, the kind

4
00:00:11,046 --> 00:00:14,028
that happens only a few times in millions of

5
00:00:14,028 --> 00:00:17,349
years. What would happen to the park, to America,

6
00:00:17,349 --> 00:00:19,856
and to the rest of the world? And how

7
00:00:19,856 --> 00:00:22,973
worried should you really be? Stay to the end,

8
00:00:22,973 --> 00:00:26,091
because the real answer surprises most people.

9
00:00:26,641 --> 00:00:30,312
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:30,312 --> 00:00:34,861
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

11
00:00:34,861 --> 00:00:37,734
going inside America's supervolcano.

12
00:00:38,284 --> 00:00:43,094
Yellowstone was the world's first national park, created in

13
00:00:43,094 --> 00:00:47,008
1872. Millions of people visit every year to see

14
00:00:47,008 --> 00:00:51,737
geysers, bubbling mud and rainbow colored hot springs. But

15
00:00:51,737 --> 00:00:55,324
all of that exists for one reason: there's a

16
00:00:55,324 --> 00:00:58,423
huge volcanic system right underneath.

17
00:00:58,973 --> 00:01:03,754
Yellowstone has more than ten thousand hydrothermal features, about

18
00:01:03,754 --> 00:01:07,036
half of all the geysers on Earth. Old Faithful

19
00:01:07,036 --> 00:01:10,889
erupts roughly every hour and a half, shooting boiling

20
00:01:10,889 --> 00:01:13,458
water dozens of meters into the air.

21
00:01:14,008 --> 00:01:18,149
Those rainbow colors at Grand Prismatic Spring aren't paint

22
00:01:18,149 --> 00:01:22,360
or minerals. They come from heat-loving microbes. Each color

23
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:26,290
lives in a different temperature ring, from the scalding

24
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:29,027
blue center to the cooler orange edges.

25
00:01:29,577 --> 00:01:33,489
And Yellowstone is home to Steamboat Geyser, the tallest

26
00:01:33,489 --> 00:01:37,331
active geyser in the world. Its biggest eruptions shoot

27
00:01:37,331 --> 00:01:40,754
water more than ninety meters high, taller than a

28
00:01:40,754 --> 00:01:42,291
thirty-story building.

29
00:01:42,841 --> 00:01:45,905
The heat comes from a hotspot, a plume of

30
00:01:45,905 --> 00:01:49,416
hot rock rising from deep inside the Earth, the

31
00:01:49,416 --> 00:01:52,704
same kind of thing that built Hawaii. As the

32
00:01:52,704 --> 00:01:56,589
North American Plate slides over it, the hotspot has

33
00:01:56,589 --> 00:01:59,801
left a trail of old volcanoes across Idaho.

34
00:02:00,351 --> 00:02:05,731
Yellowstone has had three huge, caldera-forming eruptions. The first,

35
00:02:05,731 --> 00:02:09,318
about two point one million years ago, was one

36
00:02:09,318 --> 00:02:12,982
of the largest volcanic eruptions we know of on

37
00:02:12,982 --> 00:02:13,450
Earth.

38
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,822
The second came about one point three million years

39
00:02:17,822 --> 00:02:21,793
ago. The third, about six hundred thirty one thousand

40
00:02:21,793 --> 00:02:25,765
years ago, created the giant crater, or caldera, that

41
00:02:25,765 --> 00:02:29,137
much of the park sits in today, roughly forty

42
00:02:29,137 --> 00:02:31,835
five by eighty five kilometers wide.

43
00:02:32,385 --> 00:02:35,651
How big was that first eruption? It threw out

44
00:02:35,651 --> 00:02:39,788
around two thousand five hundred cubic kilometers of rock

45
00:02:39,788 --> 00:02:43,344
and ash. The 1980 eruption of Mount Saint Helens,

46
00:02:43,344 --> 00:02:47,626
which flattened forests for miles, released about one. That

47
00:02:47,626 --> 00:02:52,779
makes Yellowstone's biggest eruption roughly two thousand times larger.

48
00:02:53,329 --> 00:02:57,821
Scientists call an eruption this size a super-eruption: more

49
00:02:57,821 --> 00:03:01,938
than one thousand cubic kilometers of material at once.

50
00:03:01,938 --> 00:03:05,381
Only a handful of volcanoes on Earth have ever

51
00:03:05,381 --> 00:03:05,980
done it.

52
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:11,091
The most recent super-eruption anywhere on Earth happened about

53
00:03:11,091 --> 00:03:15,072
twenty-six thousand five hundred years ago, at Taupō in

54
00:03:15,072 --> 00:03:18,909
New Zealand. Today, that volcano is a beautiful lake.

55
00:03:18,909 --> 00:03:22,963
No human has ever witnessed a super-eruption with modern

56
00:03:22,963 --> 00:03:24,194
science watching.

57
00:03:24,744 --> 00:03:27,387
The caldera is so big that for a long

58
00:03:27,387 --> 00:03:31,458
time people didn't even realize they were standing inside

59
00:03:31,458 --> 00:03:34,387
a volcano. It's easier to see from above.

60
00:03:34,937 --> 00:03:38,434
Quick question. Have you ever been to Yellowstone, or

61
00:03:38,434 --> 00:03:41,668
would you go knowing it's a supervolcano? Tell me

62
00:03:41,668 --> 00:03:42,723
in the comments.

63
00:03:43,273 --> 00:03:47,082
So let's imagine the worst case. A super-eruption like

64
00:03:47,082 --> 00:03:50,326
the one two million years ago. It would likely

65
00:03:50,326 --> 00:03:54,347
start with weeks or months of warning signs: earthquakes,

66
00:03:54,347 --> 00:03:57,097
the ground rising, and new steam vents.

67
00:03:57,647 --> 00:04:01,303
Then, the explosion. Huge columns of ash and gas

68
00:04:01,303 --> 00:04:05,491
would shoot tens of kilometers into the sky. Avalanches

69
00:04:05,491 --> 00:04:09,908
of scorching gas and rock, called pyroclastic flows, would

70
00:04:09,908 --> 00:04:14,629
race across the land around the caldera, destroying everything

71
00:04:14,629 --> 00:04:15,695
in their path.

72
00:04:16,245 --> 00:04:19,061
Near the park, the damage would be total. But

73
00:04:19,061 --> 00:04:21,813
the biggest effects would come from the ash.

74
00:04:22,363 --> 00:04:26,071
Computer models by the US Geological Survey show that

75
00:04:26,071 --> 00:04:29,778
ash from a super-eruption could spread across much of

76
00:04:29,778 --> 00:04:33,346
the United States. Places near the volcano could be

77
00:04:33,346 --> 00:04:36,424
buried under more than a meter of ash, while

78
00:04:36,424 --> 00:04:40,411
cities hundreds of kilometers away could get centimeters.

79
00:04:40,961 --> 00:04:44,408
Even a few centimeters of volcanic ash is a

80
00:04:44,408 --> 00:04:48,256
big problem. It's not soft like smoke. It's made

81
00:04:48,256 --> 00:04:51,463
of tiny pieces of rock and glass. It can

82
00:04:51,463 --> 00:04:56,513
collapse roofs, ruin crops, clog engines and contaminate water.

83
00:04:57,063 --> 00:04:59,898
We know how far it can travel, because it

84
00:04:59,898 --> 00:05:04,115
has happened before. Layers of ash from Yellowstone's ancient

85
00:05:04,115 --> 00:05:07,503
eruptions have been found in rocks across much of

86
00:05:07,503 --> 00:05:11,375
the western and central United States, hundreds and even

87
00:05:11,375 --> 00:05:14,002
thousands of kilometers from the park.

88
00:05:14,552 --> 00:05:18,610
Flights would stop across large areas. When a much

89
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:22,749
smaller volcano erupted in Iceland in 2010, it shut

90
00:05:22,749 --> 00:05:26,157
down much of European air travel for days.

91
00:05:26,707 --> 00:05:30,580
And it wouldn't stop at America. A super-eruption would

92
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:33,820
blast huge amounts of sulfur gas high into the

93
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:38,256
atmosphere. That gas forms tiny droplets that reflect sunlight,

94
00:05:38,256 --> 00:05:41,918
which could cool the whole planet for several years.

95
00:05:42,468 --> 00:05:46,607
We've seen a smaller version of this. In 1815,

96
00:05:46,607 --> 00:05:51,646
Mount Tambora in Indonesia erupted. The next year, 1816,

97
00:05:51,646 --> 00:05:55,695
became known as the Year Without a Summer. It

98
00:05:55,695 --> 00:05:59,924
snowed in New England in June, and crops failed

99
00:05:59,924 --> 00:06:02,713
across the Northern Hemisphere.

100
00:06:03,263 --> 00:06:07,419
A Yellowstone super-eruption would be far larger than Tambora,

101
00:06:07,419 --> 00:06:10,837
so farming around the world could suffer for years.

102
00:06:11,387 --> 00:06:14,126
If an eruption were coming, where would you go?

103
00:06:14,126 --> 00:06:15,525
Tell me in the comments.

104
00:06:16,075 --> 00:06:20,696
Now the good news. Scientists at the Yellowstone Volcano

105
00:06:20,696 --> 00:06:26,720
Observatory watch the volcano constantly, with seismometers, GPS stations

106
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:27,958
and satellites.

107
00:06:28,508 --> 00:06:32,281
They record between one thousand five hundred and two

108
00:06:32,281 --> 00:06:36,979
thousand five hundred small earthquakes in Yellowstone every year.

109
00:06:36,979 --> 00:06:39,826
Most are too weak to feel. The ground of

110
00:06:39,826 --> 00:06:42,745
the caldera also rises and falls by a few

111
00:06:42,745 --> 00:06:47,087
centimeters as water and gas move underground. To scientists,

112
00:06:47,087 --> 00:06:49,649
that's a volcano breathing normally.

113
00:06:50,199 --> 00:06:53,993
The US Geological Survey estimates the yearly chance of

114
00:06:53,993 --> 00:06:58,062
another super-eruption at about one in seven hundred thirty

115
00:06:58,062 --> 00:07:01,993
thousand. That's lower than many people's chance of being

116
00:07:01,993 --> 00:07:04,407
hit by lightning in their lifetime.

117
00:07:04,957 --> 00:07:08,393
And Yellowstone is not overdue. You may have heard

118
00:07:08,393 --> 00:07:12,379
it erupts every six hundred thousand years. But scientists

119
00:07:12,379 --> 00:07:15,746
say volcanoes don't work on a schedule, and three

120
00:07:15,746 --> 00:07:18,632
eruptions aren't enough to find a pattern.

121
00:07:19,182 --> 00:07:23,551
Even better, studies show the magma chamber under Yellowstone

122
00:07:23,551 --> 00:07:27,204
is mostly solid. Only about five to fifteen percent

123
00:07:27,204 --> 00:07:30,356
of it is molten. It's not clear there's even

124
00:07:30,356 --> 00:07:34,009
enough liquid magma for a super-eruption right now.

125
00:07:34,559 --> 00:07:38,641
So what is more likely? A hydrothermal explosion, when

126
00:07:38,641 --> 00:07:42,647
underground water flashes into steam and blasts out a

127
00:07:42,647 --> 00:07:47,182
crater. These happen in Yellowstone every few years, usually

128
00:07:47,182 --> 00:07:47,636
small.

129
00:07:48,186 --> 00:07:51,800
Another possibility is a lava flow. Since the last

130
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:56,498
super-eruption, Yellowstone has had dozens of eruptions of thick,

131
00:07:56,498 --> 00:08:00,329
slow lava. The most recent was about seventy thousand

132
00:08:00,329 --> 00:08:03,437
years ago. A new one would be dramatic, but

133
00:08:03,437 --> 00:08:06,255
it would mostly affect the park itself.

134
00:08:06,805 --> 00:08:10,412
In July 2024, one of these blasts at Biscuit

135
00:08:10,412 --> 00:08:14,183
Basin threw rocks and steam into the air, near

136
00:08:14,183 --> 00:08:18,445
a crowd of visitors on the boardwalk. Thankfully, no

137
00:08:18,445 --> 00:08:20,331
one was seriously hurt.

138
00:08:20,881 --> 00:08:24,696
That's the real danger for visitors today: the boiling

139
00:08:24,696 --> 00:08:27,735
water. The ground near hot springs can be a

140
00:08:27,735 --> 00:08:31,409
thin crust over scalding water, which is why rangers

141
00:08:31,409 --> 00:08:34,235
tell everyone to stay on the boardwalks.

142
00:08:34,785 --> 00:08:39,344
So, what if Yellowstone erupted tomorrow? A super-eruption would

143
00:08:39,344 --> 00:08:42,834
be one of the biggest disasters in human history.

144
00:08:42,834 --> 00:08:46,182
But the chance of it happening in your lifetime

145
00:08:46,182 --> 00:08:47,607
is incredibly small.

146
00:08:48,157 --> 00:08:52,492
For now, the supervolcano mostly does something beautiful. It

147
00:08:52,492 --> 00:08:56,187
powers the geysers, colors the hot springs and makes

148
00:08:56,187 --> 00:08:59,670
Yellowstone one of the most amazing places on our

149
00:08:59,670 --> 00:09:00,167
planet.

150
00:09:00,717 --> 00:09:04,686
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

151
00:09:04,686 --> 00:09:06,328
Tell me in the comments.

152
00:09:06,878 --> 00:09:10,591
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

153
00:09:10,591 --> 00:09:13,697
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

154
00:09:13,697 --> 00:09:17,342
friend who's worried about Yellowstone. See you in the

155
00:09:17,342 --> 00:09:17,950
next one.

